        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434539",
          "text": "You are building a $k$-NN classifier to filter spam emails. You divide 100 labeled emails into a training set ( 60 emails), a validation set ( 20 emails), and a test set ( 20 emails). For $k=1$, you get a training error of $0.0 \\%$ and a validation error of $11.2 \\%$. For $k=2$, you get a training error of $8.5 \\%$ and a validation error of $9.4 \\%$. Which classifier should you expect to have a lower error on the test set, and why?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> The $k=1$ classifier, because it has a $0.0 \\%$ training error.</li><li> The $k=2$ classifier, because it has a lower validation error.</li> <li>The $k=1$ classifier, because it will generalize better with less training error.</li>  <li>Both classifiers are equally likely to have the same error on the test set.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,one-mark",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434535",
          "text": "Given the following training set of four labeled points:<br />$$<br />(-0.8,0),(-0.4,1),(0.2,1),(0.8,0)<br />$$<br />What is the decision boundary of a 1-NN classifier using this training set?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> <br />$$<br />h(x)= \\begin{cases}1 & \\text { if }-0.4 \\leq x \\leq 0.8 \\\\ 0 & \\text { otherwise }\\end{cases}<br />$$</li><li> <br />$$<br />h(x)= \\begin{cases}1 & \\text { if }-0.8 \\leq x \\leq 0.2 \\\\ 0 & \\text { otherwise }\\end{cases}<br />$$</li> <li><br />$$<br />h(x)= \\begin{cases}1 & \\text { if }-0.6 \\leq x \\leq 0.5 \\\\ 0 & \\text { if } x<-0.6 \\text { or } x>0.5\\end{cases}<br />$$</li>  <li><br />$$<br />h(x)= \\begin{cases}1 & \\text { if } 0.2 \\leq x \\leq 0.8 \\\\ 0 & \\text { otherwise }\\end{cases}<br />$$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,two-marks",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434542",
          "text": "<p>Imagine you are using a $k$-Nearest Neighbor classifier on a dataset with lots of noise. You want your classifier to be less sensitive to the noise. Which of the following is likely to help and with what side effect?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>Increase the value of $k \\rightarrow$ Increase in prediction time</li><li>Decrease the value of $k \\rightarrow$ Increase in prediction time</li><li>Increase the value of $k \\rightarrow$ Decrease in prediction time</li><li>Decrease the value of $k \\rightarrow$ Decrease in prediction time</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,one-mark",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434531",
          "text": "<p>The table below shows the test set for a<strong> 1-nearest-neighbor classifier</strong> that uses Manhattan distance, i.e., the distance between two points at coordinates $p$ and $q$ is $|p-q|$. The only attribute, $X$, is real-valued, and the label, $Y$, has two classes, 0 and 1 . Suppose a subset containing $n \\leq 8$ examples is selected from this set to train the classifier, and the accuracy of the classifier is 100 percent when tested on this set (with all 8 examples). What is the smallest possible value for $n$ ? In case of ties in distance, use the example with smallest $X$ value as the neighbor.<br> </p><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width: 500px; border-spacing: 1px;\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">X</th><td>-5</td><td>-4</td><td>-1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Y</th><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table><p><br> </p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>2</li><li>3</li><li>4</li><li>5</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,one-mark",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434525",
          "text": "In $k$-nearest neighbor ( kNN ) regression, the prediction of $Y$ at point $x_{0}$ is given by the average of the values $Y$ at the $k$ neighbors closest to $x_{0}$. Denote the $\\ell$-nearest neighbor to $x_{0}$ by $x_{(\\ell)}$ and its corresponding $Y$ value by $y_{(\\ell)}$. Which of the following expresses the prediction $\\hat{f}\\left(x_{0}\\right)$ in terms of $y_{(\\ell)}$, for $1 \\leq \\ell \\leq k$ ?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $\\hat{f}\\left(x_{0}\\right)=\\frac{1}{k} \\sum_{\\ell=1}^{k} y_{(\\ell)}$</li><li> $\\hat{f}\\left(x_{0}\\right)=\\frac{1}{k} \\sum_{\\ell=1}^{k}\\left(y_{(\\ell)} \\cdot e^{-\\left|x_{(\\ell)}-x_{0}\\right|}\\right)$</li> <li>$\\hat{f}\\left(x_{0}\\right)=\\frac{1}{k} \\sum_{\\ell=1}^{k}\\left(y_{(\\ell)}+\\left(x_{(\\ell)}-x_{0}\\right)^{2}\\right)$</li>  <li>$\\hat{f}\\left(x_{0}\\right)=\\frac{1}{k} \\sum_{\\ell=1}^{k}\\left(y_{(\\ell)}-\\left(x_{(\\ell)}-x_{0}\\right)\\right)$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,one-mark",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434541",
          "text": "<p>Figure shows the decision boundaries (DB) for two nearest-neighbour classifiers - 1NN and 3NN. Consider following statements and choose the correct option (True/False for all the statements 1/2/3/4).<br><img alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"><br><br>1. DB1 belongs to $3 N N$ while DB2 belongs to $1 N N$.<br>2. DB1 belongs to $1 N N$ while DB2 belongs to $3 N N$.<br>3. DB1 gives zero test error.<br>4. DB1 gives zero training error.</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>1: True, 2: False, 3: True, 4: False</li><li>1: False, 2: True, 3: False, 4: True</li><li>1: False, 2: True, 3: True, 4: False</li><li>1: True, 2: False, 3: False, 4: True</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,one-mark",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434533",
          "text": "A 1-NN classifier will have non-zero training error on a data set with three points $(x, y)$ if:<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> This is not possible for a 1-NN classifier with any data set.</li><li> Two or more points have the same $x$-value but different labels.</li> <li>The points are linearly separable.</li>  <li>The distance between all points is equal .</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,two-marks",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434544",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following training set in a two-dimensional feature space:</p><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width: 500px; border-spacing: 1px;\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">(x , y)</th><th scope=\"col\">Class</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(-1 , 1)</td><td><p>-</p></td></tr><tr><td>(0 , 1)</td><td>&nbsp;+</td></tr><tr><td>(0, 2)</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>(1 , -1)</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>(1 , 0)</td><td>+</td></tr><tr><td>(1 , 2)</td><td>+</td></tr><tr><td>(2 , 2)</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>(2 , 3)</td><td>+</td></tr></tbody></table><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"287\" height=\"280\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"><br><br>What are the predictions of the k-nearest-neighbor classifier at the point $(1,1)$ for different values of $k$ ?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>3-nearest: +, 5-nearest: +, 7-nearest: -</li><li>3-nearest: -, 5-nearest: -, 7-nearest: -</li><li>3-nearest: +, 5-nearest: -, 7-nearest: +</li><li>3-nearest: -, 5-nearest: +, 7-nearest: +</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,one-mark",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434529",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following statements about $k$-Nearest Neighbor ( $k$-NN) are true in a classification setting, and for all $k$ ? Select all that apply.</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>The training error of a 1-NN will always be better than or equal to that of $5-\\mathrm{NN}$.</li><li>The test error of a 1-NN will always be better than that of a 5-NN.</li><li>The decision boundary of the k-NN classifier is linear.</li><li>The time needed to classify a test example with the k-NN classifier grows with the size of the training set.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a;d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,multiple-selects,one-mark",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434526",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following is true about the k-nearest neighbors (KNN) algorithm?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>It is a parametric model.</li><li>It can learn&nbsp;a nonlinear decision boundary between classes.</li><li>It requires a separate training phase and testing phase for prediction.</li><li>It typically requires longer training compared to other ML algorithms.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,one-mark",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434543",
          "text": "Let $S_{1}$ and $S_{2}$ be two separate training data sets. Let $h_{k}\\left(S_{i}, x\\right)$ be the binary classifier function which takes in a training data set $S_{i}$ and a test example $x$, and outputs the result of the $k$-nearest neighbor classifier that has been trained on data set $S_{i}$ on the test example $x$. (So, for example, $h_{5}\\left(S_{1}, x\\right)$ is the output of the 5-nearest neighbor classifier trained on $S_{1}$ on the test example $x$ ). Assume that the two labels are 0 and 1. Consider the following two statements:<br />(a) If $h_{1}\\left(S_{1}, x\\right)=1$ and $h_{1}\\left(S_{2}, x\\right)=1$, then $h_{1}\\left(S_{1} \\cup S_{2}, x\\right)=1$<br />(b) If $h_{3}\\left(S_{1}, x\\right)=1$ and $h_{3}\\left(S_{2}, x\\right)=1$, then $h_{3}\\left(S_{1} \\cup S_{2}, x\\right)=1$<br />Which of the following statements is correct?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> Only (a) is correct.</li><li> Only (b) is correct.</li> <li>Both (a) and (b) are correct.</li>  <li>Both (a) and (b) are incorrect.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "A",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,two-marks",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434532",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following statements is/are TRUE about the k-NN classifier?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>If point $A$ is among point B's k-nearest neighbors, then point $B$ is always among point $A$ 's k-nearest neighbors.</li><li>On a training set with 1000 positive items and 1000 negative items, it is possible for 1-NN to have a training set accuracy of $0 \\%$, given that there is no overlap in points.</li><li>Multiplying each dimension of every item's feature vector by 0.1 (assuming Euclidean distance) will not change k-NN results.</li><li>As the value of $k$ used in a k-NN classifier is incrementally increased from 1 to n , the total number of training examples, the classification accuracy on the training set will always increase.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "C",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434537",
          "text": "You are using a 1-NN classifier to filter spam emails. Given a training set of size $n$ and a validation set of size $m$, and assuming that the distance calculation takes constant time, what is the asymptotic time complexity of computing the validation error? (Use big-O notation in terms of $n$ and $m$.)<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $O(n)$</li><li> $O(m)$</li> <li>$O(n+m)$</li>  <li>$O(n \\cdot m)$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,one-mark",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434545",
          "text": "Consider a dataset with two features and three points: $x_{q}=(0,0)$ is the query point, and the other points are $x_{1}=(1,1)$ and $x_{2}=(1.5,0)$. The task is to determine the 1-Nearest-Neighbor (1-NN) for the query point $x_{q}$ using two different distance metrics: Euclidean and Manhattan distances.<br />Which of the following statements is true about the nearest neighbor of the query point $x_{q}$ based on the distance metric used?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $x_{1}$ is the nearest neighbor according to both Euclidean and Manhattan distances.</li><li> $x_{2}$ is the nearest neighbor according to both Euclidean and Manhattan distances.</li> <li>$x_{1}$ is the nearest neighbor according to Euclidean distance, and $x_{2}$ is the nearest neighbor according to Manhattan distance.</li>  <li>$x_{2}$ is the nearest neighbor according to Euclidean distance, and $x_{1}$ is the nearest neighbor according to Manhattan distance.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,one-mark",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "434527",
          "text": "<p>You are using a<strong> Probabilistic 3NN</strong> model for binary classification with labels $\\{-1,+1\\}$. The model predicts the label of a query point based on the relative proportion of labels among its 3 nearest neighbors. For example, if the 3 nearest neighbors have labels $\\{-1,-1,+1\\}$, the model assigns a probability of $2 / 3$ to label -1 and $1 / 3$ to label +1 .<br>Given the 5 nearest neighbors and their distances from the query point $q$ :</p><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width: 500px; border-spacing: 1px;\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Neighbor</th><th scope=\"col\">Distance from q</th><th scope=\"col\">Label</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>N1</td><td>1.2</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>N2</td><td>1.5</td><td>+1</td></tr><tr><td>N3</td><td>1.7</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>N4</td><td>2.0</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>N5</td><td>2.5</td><td>+1</td></tr></tbody></table><p><br>What is the predicted probability $P(y=-1)$ using the 3 nearest neighbors?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$1 / 3$</li><li>$2 / 3$</li><li>$3 / 4$</li><li>$1 / 2$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-ml-2,goclasses,machine-learning,two-marks",
          "category": "Machine Learning"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}